backing data ptr: c0000240f0
✓ slice1 shares backing array with backing
✓ slice2 shares backing array with backing

=== Testing Pointer to Slice Element ===
Original slice: [1 2 3 4 5]
Pointer value: 3 (address: 0xc000020130)
Element address: 0xc000020130
✓ Pointer points to slice element
After modification via pointer: [1 2 100 4 5]

=== Testing Pointer Chains ===
value: 42 (addr: 0xc0000121c8)
ptr1: 0xc0000121c8 -> 42 (addr: 0xc00004c028)
ptr2: 0xc00004c028 -> 0xc0000121c8 -> 42 (addr: 0xc00004c030)
ptr3: 0xc00004c030 -> 0xc00004c028 -> 0xc0000121c8 -> 42
✓ Pointer chain is correct

=== Testing Struct with Pointers ===
Node.Value: 42
Node.Slice: [1 2 3]
Node.Ptr: 0xc00002a068 -> 2
Slice[1] addr: 0xc00002a068
✓ Pointer points to slice element within struct

=== Testing Multiple References to Same Slice ===
Slice1 data ptr: c000020150
Slice2 data ptr: c000020150
*Ptr data ptr: c000020150
✓ All three references point to the same backing array
After Slice1[0] = 100:
  Slice1: [100 2 3 4 5]
  Slice2: [100 2 3 4 5]
  *Ptr: [100 2 3 4 5]

=== Summary ===
These are the memory structure patterns that MUST be preserved during serialization/deserialization:
1. Slices that share the same backing array must continue to share it after deserialization
2. Pointers to slice elements must point to the actual deserialized slice elements
3. Pointer chains must maintain their structure (ptr -> ptr -> ptr -> value)
4. Structs with pointers to their own fields must maintain those relationships
5. Multiple references to the same slice must all reference the same slice after deserialization
